Serve docs/ as static files — do not run Jekyll over it.

index.html is a single self-contained page with no build step and no template
syntax, so a Jekyll pass buys nothing here.

What it can cost: Jekyll also walks the Markdown files next to this one and
parses them as Liquid templates. None of them contains `{{` or `{%` today —
checked — but the theme documentation is about writing templates, and the day
one of those braces appears inside a code fence, Jekyll treats it as a template
tag and fails the build. That turns an unrelated documentation edit into a
broken site, with the error surfacing in Pages rather than anywhere the author
was looking.

GitHub only tests whether this file exists; its contents are ignored.
